<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/foundational/biological-molecules">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is a beginner lesson in Biology. It needs zero prior knowledge — not
    even chemistry. If you can read a food label and stir a cup of tea, you
    already own every tool this lesson uses.
  </p>
  <p>By the end of this lesson you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>name the four families of molecules every living thing is built from;</li>
    <li>say which chemical elements each family contains;</li>
    <li>explain how big molecules are built from small units and taken apart again;</li>
    <li>name the five classic food tests and read their colour results;</li>
    <li>explain why fat holds about twice the energy per gram that carbohydrate does.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Start with your last meal</h2>
  <p>
    Think of whatever you ate today. Suppose it was bread, butter and a boiled
    egg. Imagine grinding it finer and finer — past flour, past dust, past
    anything a microscope can see. Eventually you reach
    <strong>molecules</strong>: the smallest pieces a substance can be broken
    into and still be that substance. A molecule is a cluster of atoms — atoms
    being the tiniest units of ordinary matter — stuck together.
  </p>
  <p>
    Here is the surprise: that varied meal is built from just four families
    of molecule. The bread is mostly one family, the butter almost
    entirely another, the egg white mostly a third. Your body is built from
    the same four, and biologists call them <strong>carbohydrates</strong>,
    <strong>fats</strong>, <strong>proteins</strong> and <strong>DNA</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why care which family a molecule belongs to? Because the family predicts
    the job. Fast fuel is carbohydrate. Long-term storage is fat. Working
    machinery is protein. The instruction archive is DNA.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> food and bodies are made of molecules, and four
    families do all the work. Next: how to tell them apart.
  </p>

  <h2>Telling the families apart: their elements</h2>
  <p>
    Chemists sort matter into <strong>elements</strong> — basic substances
    such as carbon, hydrogen, oxygen, nitrogen and phosphorus, which cannot be
    split into anything simpler. Every molecule is a recipe of elements.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Carbohydrates</strong> and <strong>fats</strong> contain carbon,
    hydrogen and oxygen. <strong>Proteins</strong> add nitrogen to that list —
    and usually a pinch of sulfur. <strong>DNA</strong> adds phosphorus. No
    nitrogen, not a protein — the rule is that reliable.
  </p>
  <p>
    The families also share one building habit. Every big molecule in this
    lesson is assembled from small repeating units, like a wall built from
    identical bricks. <strong>Starch, glycogen and cellulose</strong> are all
    chains of one brick, the sugar <strong>glucose</strong>.
    <strong>Proteins</strong> are chains of <strong>amino acids</strong>.
    <strong>Fats and oils</strong> are built from <strong>fatty acids and
    glycerol</strong>. DNA is built from <strong>nucleotides</strong>. Enzymes
    — proteins that speed up a chemical change without being used up
    themselves — join the bricks and split them apart again, all day long.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "chemical" means artificial or dangerous. A chemical is simply any substance made of molecules — sugar, water and every part of you included. Nothing in this lesson is artificial.</p>

  <h2>Carbohydrates: fuel now, fuel later, and scaffolding</h2>
  <p>
    Start with the sugar you stir into tea. That sugar is
    <strong>sucrose</strong>, a <strong>carbohydrate</strong> — a molecule of
    carbon, hydrogen and oxygen, with the hydrogen and oxygen in the same
    two-to-one ratio as water. Early chemists wrote the general formula as
    C<sub>x</sub>(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>y</sub>, literally "watered carbon".
    Carbohydrates are sugars and the chains built from sugars.
  </p>
  <p>
    The single sugars — <strong>monosaccharides</strong>, meaning "one sugar"
    — are the transportable fuel. <strong>Glucose</strong> is the one that
    matters most: it is what respiration burns and what your blood carries to
    every cell. <strong>Fructose</strong>, the sugar in fruit, is its sweeter
    cousin.
  </p>
  <p>
    Join two sugars and you get a <strong>disaccharide</strong> — "two
    sugars". <strong>Sucrose</strong> is glucose joined to fructose — the
    sugar plants ship in their sap. <strong>Lactose</strong> in milk is
    glucose joined to galactose.
  </p>
  <p>
    Long chains are <strong>polysaccharides</strong> — "many sugars". Three
    repay a close look: identical glucose bricks can build three utterly
    different buildings.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Starch</strong> is the compact store that plants fill their
    potatoes and seeds with. <strong>Glycogen</strong> is the animal version,
    branched more heavily so glucose can be snapped off quickly from many
    ends; your liver and muscles hold about 400–500 g — roughly enough
    fuel to reach a marathon's halfway mark. <strong>Cellulose</strong> joins
    the same glucose units with bonds of a different geometry (chemists call
    them beta linkages, and human enzymes cannot break them). Straight chains
    grip each other into cables — the fibre of every plant cell wall. We
    cannot digest it; trees stand on it.
  </p>
  <p>
    One brick, three linkages, three fates: instant fuel, medium-term store,
    structural scaffolding.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> plants store cellulose. Cellulose is the structural cable — cell walls, fibre, scaffolding; the storage polymer is starch, packed into potatoes and seeds precisely because its linkages can be opened again. Both are glucose chains, and the whole difference is the geometry of one bond — which is also why we can digest the store and not the wall.</p>

  <h2>Proteins: the machinery family</h2>
  <p>
    Egg white is protein — and so are the enzymes in your saliva, the
    haemoglobin in your blood and the keratin of your hair. A <strong>protein</strong> is
    a chain of <strong>amino acids</strong> — small molecules that share one
    backbone but each carry a different side group. There are twenty standard
    kinds, and cells string them in any order. String a hundred in any order
    and the number of possible chains dwarfs the number of atoms in the
    universe — which is how one family can do so many jobs.
  </p>
  <p>
    The order of the beads is the <strong>primary structure</strong>. Then the
    chain folds. Weak grips — hydrogen bonds — coil it into helices and
    sheets: the <strong>secondary structure</strong>. The side groups pack it
    into a precise three-dimensional shape: the <strong>tertiary
    structure</strong>. Separate chains can dock together: the
    <strong>quaternary structure</strong> — haemoglobin is four chains in one
    machine.
  </p>
  <p>
    The shape <em>is</em> the job. Enzymes fold into an <strong>active
    site</strong> — a pocket whose shape fits exactly one target molecule —
    and speed reactions up about a millionfold while staying unchanged.
    Keratin and collagen fold into fibres for hair and tendon. Haemoglobin
    folds into a gripper that carries oxygen in your blood. Insulin is a
    <strong>peptide hormone</strong> — a short chain carrying a message
    between organs. Antibodies fold to recognise shapes they have never met.
  </p>
  <p>
    Heat, or the wrong <strong>pH</strong> (the measure of how acid something
    is), shakes the weak bonds loose and the shape unravels —
    <strong>denaturation</strong>. That is why a boiled egg never unboils, and
    why a fever towards 41 °C is dangerous: your enzymes are approaching the
    edge of their shapes.
  </p>
  <p>
    Digestion is chain-cutting done by proteins themselves.
    Proteases snip proteins into amino acids. Amylase snips starch into
    maltose. Lipase splits fats into fatty acids and glycerol. Big insoluble
    chains become small soluble units — the universal job of digestion.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> protein is the body's preferred fuel. Its day job is structure and machinery — enzymes, muscle, antibodies — and it is the fuel of last resort, drawn on seriously only when carbohydrate and fat stores are stressed, at the cost of breaking down tissue. The 17 kJ/g figure on the dietitian's chart is what protein yields when burned, not what it is for.</p>

  <h2>Fats and oils: the dense fuel</h2>
  <p>
    Butter and olive oil are <strong>lipids</strong> — the family chemists
    split into fats (solid at room temperature) and oils (liquid). Both are
    built from <strong>fatty acids and glycerol</strong>: one glycerol
    backbone holding three fatty-acid tails. The whole assembly is called a
    <strong>triglyceride</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why does the body bank fat rather than starch for lean times? Two
    reasons, both from chemistry. Fat holds about twice the energy per gram —
    roughly 39 kJ/g against 17 kJ/g — because its carbon is already rich in
    hydrogen. And it is stored dry: a gram of glycogen drags about 3 g of
    water along with it; fat stores without water. That is why seeds pack
    oil, migrating birds fuel on fat, and your body keeps its reserves as
    fat.
  </p>
  <p>
    You have seen the two kinds on food labels. <strong>Saturated</strong>
    fatty acids have no carbon–carbon double bonds, so their straight chains
    pack tightly — solid butter and lard — and they are the kind associated
    with raising blood cholesterol. <strong>Unsaturated</strong> fats (olive
    oil, fish oils) have kinks at their double bonds that keep them liquid —
    the "healthier" side of the label.
  </p>
  <p>
    The family has one more starring role. Swap one tail for a phosphate head
    and you get a <strong>phospholipid</strong> — a molecule with a
    water-loving head and water-hating tails. Trillions of them line up into
    double-layer sheets, and those sheets are every cell membrane you own;
    that membrane has its own lesson later in this course. One footnote:
    vitamins A, D, E and K are fat-soluble — the reason a salad dressed with
    a little oil actually delivers its vitamins.
  </p>

  <h2>Water: where all of this chemistry happens</h2>
  <p>
    None of it runs on dry molecules. Water makes up about 70% of a cell, and
    digestion, transport and excretion all happen dissolved in it. Water earns
    the job by being an extraordinary <strong>solvent</strong> — a liquid
    that other things dissolve into. Its molecules are
    <strong>polar</strong>: slightly positive at one end and slightly negative
    at the other, like tiny magnets. That lets them pry apart ionic and polar
    substances and hold the pieces apart.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sucrose — your table sugar — is one of the most soluble common solids
    known. The graph shows the practical consequence: hot water dissolves
    nearly three times as much sugar as cold.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="solubility of sucrose in water against temperature (CRC Handbook data) — hot water dissolves nearly three times more sugar than cold" widget="plotter" config={solubilityCfg} />

  <p>
    Now trace the roles through one meal. <strong>Digestion</strong>: starch,
    proteins and fats are big and insoluble, so enzymes must first cut them
    into small soluble units — digestion is the chemistry of making food
    water-soluble. <strong>Transport</strong>: your blood plasma carries
    glucose, amino acids and urea in solution; phloem sap, the pipeline
    plants use to ship sugar, carries sucrose the same way — about 340 g per
    litre. <strong>Excretion</strong>: urea leaves the body
    dissolved in water — no solvent, no exit.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> four families — carbohydrate for fuel, fat for
    storage, protein for machinery, DNA for instructions — plus water, the
    solvent all their jobs need. Next: detecting each family in a lab.
  </p>

  <h2>How to identify them: the five food tests</h2>
  <p>
    You cannot see molecules, but you can test for them. Each family changes
    colour when the right reagent is added. These five tests are classics of
    the school lab and of food science; the strip below names each reagent
    and its positive result.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="five tests, five colour changes — the working detection kit of food science" widget="equationStep" config={testsCfg} />

  <p>
    A few habits keep the tests honest. Benedict's test needs real
    <em>heating</em> — a water bath at about 80 °C, never a naked flame on the
    tube. Biuret reagent goes in drop by drop, or it can give a false
    cloudiness. The ethanol emulsion test works only if the sample truly
    dissolves in the ethanol first. And run a negative control: distilled
    water should fail all five.
  </p>
  <p>
    The kit in action: a "sports bar" that turns blue-black with iodine
    (starch), brick-red with Benedict's (reducing sugars), lilac with biuret
    (protein) and cloudy with ethanol (fat) has told you its contents more
    reliably than the wrapper's marketing.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> Benedict's test detects sugar in general. It detects <em>reducing</em> sugars — glucose, maltose, fructose — while sucrose tests negative because its bond locks the reactive group away: a sucrose solution stays blue through a perfectly run test. Report "no reducing sugar", not "no sugar" — and note that hydrolysing sucrose first (boiling with dilute acid) frees glucose and fructose, which then test positive.</p>

  <h2>DNA: the molecule that instructs the others</h2>
  <p>
    One more molecule belongs here — it outranks the other three.
    <strong>DNA</strong> holds the instructions for building every protein
    above. It is built from <strong>nucleotides</strong> — units of a sugar, a
    phosphate and one of four <strong>bases</strong> — but its shape is what
    matters. Two strands coil into a <strong>double helix</strong>, like a
    ladder twisted lengthways. The rungs are base pairs held by hydrogen
    bonds, and the pairing is strict: <strong>A pairs with T, and C pairs
    with G</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    That one rule makes DNA endlessly copyable — each strand is a template
    its partner can be rebuilt from — and endlessly readable: the order of
    the bases spells, three at a time, the order of amino acids in every
    protein. Notice the division of labour. DNA is the archive. Proteins are
    the workforce. Carbohydrates and fats are the payroll and the building
    materials.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. The answers are right below.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Which element does protein contain that carbohydrate and fat do not?</li>
    <li>Why does fat hold about twice as much energy per gram as carbohydrate?</li>
    <li>A biscuit turns blue-black with iodine and lilac with biuret, but stays blue in a properly heated Benedict's test. What is in it?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Answers:</strong> (1) Nitrogen — proteins add nitrogen (and
    usually a little sulfur) to the carbon, hydrogen and oxygen the other two
    families share. (2) Its carbon is hydrogen-rich, and it is stored dry — a
    gram of glycogen drags about three grams of water with it; fat stores
    without water. (3) Starch (the iodine result) and protein (the biuret
    result), but no reducing sugar — Benedict's stays blue, and table sugar
    is one that would not show.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The deeper structure is that all four families are
    <strong>polymers built by the same reaction</strong>. A
    <em>condensation</em> reaction joins two units and releases a molecule of
    water — it makes the bond between two glucose units, the
    <strong>peptide bond</strong> between two amino acids, and the bond
    between glycerol and a fatty acid. <em>Hydrolysis</em> reverses it, adding
    water to split the bond. Digestion is hydrolysis at industrial scale, and
    condensation is why your body needs water to build with as well as to
    dissolve in.
  </p>
  <p>
    The energy economics fall out of the same chemistry. Carbohydrates are
    partly oxidised already — hence 17 kJ/g. Fats are hydrogen-rich and burn
    fully — 39 kJ/g. Protein sits between, at about 17 kJ/g usable. The
    numbers on every dietitian's chart are combustion energies, measured in a
    bomb calorimeter a century before biochemistry could explain them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three expert edges. First, <strong>enzymes as molecular
    recognition</strong>: the lock-and-key picture is really dozens of weak
    grips — hydrogen bonds and electrostatic holds — acting at once, which is
    why an enzyme's rate collapses when pH shifts the charges on its surface
    by a fraction. Second, <strong>the misfold frontier</strong>: the same
    twenty bricks can build both health and prion disease, where a protein in
    the wrong shape templates its shape onto others (mad-cow disease,
    Alzheimer's plaques) — shape, not composition, is the disease variable.
    Third, <strong>lipid biology beyond storage</strong>: cell membranes are
    fluid mosaics whose stiffness is tuned by cholesterol and tail saturation
    (cold-adapted fish make more unsaturated tails to stay flexible), and
    signalling molecules such as prostaglandins and steroid hormones are
    lipids with messages on them.
  </p>
  <p>
    One last practical shows the machinery measured, not just named. Amylase
    digests starch to maltose; starch tests blue-black with iodine, maltose
    does not. Mix amylase with starch, take a drop every 30 seconds
    into iodine, and time the first drop that stays brown — that is the
    digestion time. Now vary one factor per experiment: at 10 °C the endpoint
    takes many minutes; at 37 °C it is fastest; at 60 °C it never comes,
    because the enzyme has denatured; at pH 4 it crawls while at pH 7 it
    sprints — salivary amylase's optimum. Time-to-endpoint is the inverse of
    rate, and rate against temperature or pH traces a gentle rise followed by
    a hard collapse: molecules move faster, then the shape fails. Enzyme
    kinetics, in other words, is measurable with iodine, a water bath and a
    stopwatch.
  </p>
  <p>
    The four-family inventory is not a simplification — it is the actual
    parts list, and every branch of medicine is annotated in it.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cambridge IGCSE Biology 0610 syllabus (2026–2028), unit 4; sucrose solubility data from CRC Handbook of Chemistry and Physics; OpenStax Biology 2e, ch. 3 (Biological Macromolecules); Campbell Biology (Urry et al.), 12th ed., ch. 4–5.</p>
